module gunbc.compiler_gate_workflow

// THE REQUIRED CONTEXT READS ONE LANE (operator ruling 2026-10-09, the v1 withdrawal): "v1 is
// effectively bankrupt due to memory instability - i want to go back to pure v2 native compiler
// development - that means deleting most of our current CI jobs".
//
// This module emits .github/workflows/witnesses.yml. Until that ruling the `witnesses` aggregate read
// five lanes, and four of them judged through the v1 interpreter or judged v1's own Rust: `floor`
// (the interpreter folding the rostered witnesses), `generated` (the stage0 mirrors, the registry
// drift discriminator with its repair chain, and the lint), `rust-unit-tests`, and `seed` (the
// compiler pair built once for those three). They are DELETED here rather than retuned, because a
// lane made survivable is investment in the implementation the ruling retires. What they held is the
// declared drop gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn, and its trigger names the native
// compiler judging that population, never the return of these lanes.
//
// WHAT REMAINS IS `emit-build`: the seed emits the two retained v2 closures, cargo builds the emitted
// crates, and the built binaries are started (gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate states the subject).
// That is the one use the ruling leaves the seed. A verdict this gate gains from here is a row of
// emitted_subject_build_rows judged by a binary built from that emission, not a lane that hands the
// corpus to the v1 interpreter.
//
// THE NAME `witnesses` NOW CARRIES A DIFFERENT CONTRACT, AND THE EPOCH SAYS SO. The ruleset requires a
// context of that name and it lives outside the repository, so the aggregate keeps the id; what a
// success entitles a consumer to conclude changed materially, which is the case
// gunbc.required_ci_contract_epoch says to bump for. The epoch moved in the change that made this
// cut, so gunbc.fleet_revision_acceptance cannot admit a revision judged under the old contract as
// though it were judged under this one, nor the reverse.
//
// NO LANE THE REQUIRED CONTEXT READS RUNS ON A HOSTED RUNNER (operator ruling 2026-09-28), and that
// outlives the cut: `emit-build` and the aggregate run on the fleet, and
// compiler_gate_merge_path_runs_on_the_fleet refuses emission of a workflow in which either runs
// hosted. The ruling's receipts were merge-queue runs 36478070174 and 36464765845, where every hosted
// job waited minutes for a slot in a pool this repository does not control.
//
// SUPERSESSION CANCELS A FLEET JOB. The concurrency policy cancels a running pull-request run on the
// next push. gunbc.compile_permit_scope fleet_compile_cancellation_standing still reads NotSafe
// (producer absent), so a cancelled fleet compile may leak its ctrl-jobserver permits; this module
// does not resolve that. merge_group and heal revalidation keep their immutable groups
// (witness_floor_concurrency_group), so a later push never cancels them.

